<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gnaeus Julius Agricola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">40–93</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Agricola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gnaeus Julius Agricola was a Roman general and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from about 77 to 84 CE. He completed the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> by crushing the Ordovices and taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, then campaigned in northern Britain and defeated the Caledonians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> Graupius. His son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> wrote his biography, the Agricola, the main source for his career.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola.jpg" alt="Gnaeus Julius Agricola" width="320" height="779" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ad Meskens · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_baths_julius_agricola_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Agricola was born on 13 June 40 at the colonia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frejus">Forum Julii</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, now Fréjus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Both his grandfathers had served as imperial governors. His father, Lucius Julius Graecinus, a senator noted for his interest in philosophy, was put to death on the order of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> for refusing to prosecute the emperor's second cousin Marcus Junius Silanus. His mother was Julia Procilla, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> describes as a lady of singular virtue. Agricola was educated at Massilia (Marseille).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He served as a military tribune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> from 58 to 62 and almost certainly took part in the suppression of the Boudican uprising in 61. He was quaestor in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> in 64, tribune of the plebs in 66 and praetor in 68. In the Year of the Four Emperors he gave his support to Vespasian, and was given command of the Legio XX Valeria Victrix in Britain, where he reimposed discipline and fought the Brigantes under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Quintus Petillius Cerialis</a>. He then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Gallia Aquitania</a> for almost three years and in 76 or 77 was made suffect consul.</p><p>Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as governor in the summer of 77, he found that the Ordovices had virtually destroyed the Roman cavalry in their territory, and defeated them before subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>. In 79 he pushed his armies to the estuary of the river Taus, usually taken to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a>. He reformed the corn levy and encouraged the building of towns on the Roman model, and had the sons of native nobles given a Roman education, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> calls a means of pacifying the tribes. In 83 he faced the Caledonians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calgacus">Calgacus</a> at Mons Graupius. Tacitus puts their numbers above 30,000 and their losses at about 10,000 against 360 Roman dead, and two thirds of their army escaped into the Highlands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Agricola was recalled after an unusually long tenure. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> claims that the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> ordered this because Agricola's successes outshone his own modest victories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He was awarded triumphal decorations and a statue but never held another civil or military post, and he declined the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, from ill health or, as Tacitus claims, because of Domitian's machinations. He died on 23 August 93 on his family estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>, aged fifty-three. Rumours of poisoning by Domitian circulated, but no evidence for them was ever produced. His career is known mainly from the biography by Tacitus, his son-in-law, and from archaeological evidence in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gnaeus Julius Agricola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Vespasian the advance resumed, and <b>Gnaeus Julius Agricola</b>, governor from about 77 to 84, campaigned deep into Scotland and defeated the Caledonians at Mons Graupius in 83 or 84.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Claudius invaded Britain in 43 CE, Boudica’s revolt of 60 or 61 CE was crushed, and in 83 or 84 CE <b>Agricola</b> defeated the Caledonians at Mons Graupius, where Tacitus has their leader Calgacus say of the Romans, “they make a desert and call it peace”.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>They first appear in history when the governor <b>Agricola</b> marched north, and Tacitus says a host of Caledonian peoples gathered under Calgacus to meet him at Mons Graupius in 83 or 84.</blockquote><a href="/state/caledonii">Caledonii</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's information probably came from sailors who passed along the coast, perhaps during <b>Agricola's</b> fleet operations.</blockquote><a href="/state/creones">Creones</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Agricola's</b> campaigns of about 81 probably brought Roman troops into Galloway, and forts were built at Glenlochar and Gatehouse of Fleet.</blockquote><a href="/state/novantae">Novantae</a></li><li><blockquote>The new governor <b>Agricola</b> answered with a campaign that, Tacitus says, nearly wiped out the people, and then seized Anglesey.</blockquote><a href="/state/ordovices">Ordovices</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Forum Iulii, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.43,6.74&amp;year=74" aria-label="Forum Iulii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gnaeus Julius Agricola"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5417 3849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5417 3849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.43,6.74&amp;year=74">What was here in 74 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For myself I have long been convinced that neither for an army nor for a general is retreat safe. Better, too, is an honourable death than a life of shame, and safety and renown are for us to be found together.&#8221;</p><footer>Speech before the battle of Mons Graupius (Tacitus, Agricola, 33) &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1877 &#183; Tacitus' account; Agricola was his father-in-law &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/ancient/tacitus-agricola.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
